WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:09.984
This is the technical view of Pravrudhi-Nyaya: how a screening is built, how it's checked, and what the system does when the facts don't carry a statement.

2
00:00:09.984 --> 00:00:14.525
We'll go through the moving parts in the order a matter flows through them.

3
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:25.502
First, two names, because they get mixed up. Pravrudhi-Nyaya is the product, the app a firm opens. Prabhāsa-Nyāya is our own set of trained models, which sit inside it.

4
00:00:25.502 --> 00:00:32.832
Think of the whole system as a second reader that works before filing. It doesn't decide cases and it doesn't draft your pleadings.

5
00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:37.925
When it can't support a statement, it says so, gives the reason, and refers it to a lawyer.

6
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:45.140
Everything starts with the corpus. Every real item begins as a public judgment or an order.

7
00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:52.392
We gather it, clean it, and then two model readers go through it separately, pulling out facts and ingredient labels.

8
00:00:52.392 --> 00:01:00.512
Two readers, so that a disagreement shows up instead of hiding. Be clear about what that is: model-extracted, with no human review.

9
00:01:00.512 --> 00:01:10.119
Then the material is split three ways: training, held-out testing, and evaluation. Constructed cases exist too, but they're kept apart and always labelled.

10
00:01:10.119 --> 00:01:15.326
And evaluation items are checked against the training data, by identity and by text.

11
00:01:15.326 --> 00:01:24.375
On the real court items we have seen, that is three hundred seventy-two: three hundred thirty-six court-rejected and thirty-six court-established.

12
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:29.844
Now the judges, our trained models that read one ingredient at a time.

13
00:01:29.844 --> 00:01:35.591
An offence is broken into its ingredients, and a smaller four-billion-parameter judge scores each one.

14
00:01:35.591 --> 00:01:45.056
A larger thirty-two-billion-parameter judge then checks those same ingredients. An ingredient is shown as supported only if both judges point to a fact that carries it.

15
00:01:45.056 --> 00:01:53.958
A separate structure check then confirms every ingredient was addressed. If either is unsure, or they disagree, the answer is refer, with the reason attached.

16
00:01:53.958 --> 00:01:56.380
One ingredient at a time, two readers deep.

17
00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:07.198
On a seventy-row calibration set, on the dev stack, the four-billion judge alone agreed with the labels on thirty-three rows of seventy, against twenty-nine of seventy for refusing everything.

18
00:02:07.198 --> 00:02:12.325
That is agreement with model-extracted labels, not accuracy. A screening signal, not proof.

19
00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:21.552
Around the judges sits the harness, and this is where the decision actually gets made. Assembly of the table is deterministic.

20
00:02:21.552 --> 00:02:28.719
Every quoted passage has to appear in the source text, and a quote that doesn't is labelled not found, never invented.

21
00:02:28.719 --> 00:02:38.193
Each offence is also written as a contract in Lean, a language for exact, machine-checked reasoning, and that check confirms every ingredient was addressed.

22
00:02:38.193 --> 00:02:45.481
Unclear facts, judges that disagree, an offence outside the validated list: all of those give refer. The models propose.

23
00:02:45.481 --> 00:02:50.279
The code decides, and given the same judge answers, it gives the same decision.

24
00:02:50.279 --> 00:02:59.450
Our verifier checked a frontier model's quotes against case texts: one hundred eighty-six of two hundred eight verbatim quotes were not found verbatim.

25
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:08.677
The vocabulary of the results comes from Nyaya, a classical Indian school of logic. We borrow its discipline about what may be claimed.

26
00:03:08.677 --> 00:03:16.866
Pramana: a reason to hold something true. For us, that's a fact you can point to in the papers. Nirnaya, the settled judgment: that is withheld.

27
00:03:16.866 --> 00:03:19.994
The system never states a conclusion about your matter.

28
00:03:19.994 --> 00:03:27.898
And agama, testimony: an allegation in a complaint is a claim to be checked, and a model's output is a proposal, never evidence on its own.

29
00:03:27.898 --> 00:03:29.775
Old ideas, used as working rules.

30
00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:37.318
There's also an optional assistant, built on Claude Managed Agents, which runs Claude as an agent.

31
00:03:37.318 --> 00:03:47.449
It is optional, for signed-in users, and only when you tick the consent box. Ticking it means your facts go to Anthropic. Spending is capped per session and per day.

32
00:03:47.449 --> 00:03:55.125
It runs the same steps you'd see anyway, and shows them. But the principle doesn't change. Claude proposes. The core decides.

33
00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:06.534
The citation check is deliberately modest. It looks a citation up in an index of titles, courts, years and citation strings. That index holds no judgment text.

34
00:04:06.534 --> 00:04:15.768
So it can tell you a case exists, but not what the case says. A citation reads Found in our index only when exactly one case fits the year and the party names.

35
00:04:15.768 --> 00:04:24.363
No match, and several possible matches, are both reported as what they are. The citation check looks up a citation in our index. Found in our index.

36
00:04:24.363 --> 00:04:28.893
It does not look at quotes and does not say whether the case supports a point.

37
00:04:28.893 --> 00:04:38.650
On a constructed set of exact-text quotes, fifty-five of fifty-six were verified and none of one hundred two planted wrong quotes; that is not a rate on real citations.

38
00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:45.107
Next, the Matter tools page. On Matter tools you add the papers of a matter.

39
00:04:45.107 --> 00:04:52.661
The papers you add here are kept as text in our store, in the EU (Romania), until you delete the paper or the matter.

40
00:04:52.661 --> 00:05:03.250
The tools below use those papers, except the Section 138 checklist, which counts from dates you confirm. Two tools help with a first draft, for a lawyer to rewrite.

41
00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:15.209
On serving. A small always-on CPU service runs the harness and calls the judges. It holds no model weights. The judges run on rented serverless GPUs.

42
00:05:15.209 --> 00:05:22.101
Development, training and tests run on our own GPU machines, and results from those are labelled dev stack, local.

43
00:05:22.101 --> 00:05:31.775
On the dev stack on the Spark, thirty real-court matters took a median of three point nine seconds, one at a time. A one-machine deployment is not demonstrated.

44
00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:41.598
Finally, evaluation discipline. The test, the pass bar and the stopping rule are written down before the run, so the result can't move them.

45
00:05:41.598 --> 00:05:50.845
Every look at an evaluation set is counted, so nothing gets quietly re-run until it passes. If a result shows no difference, it's reported as no difference.

46
00:05:50.845 --> 00:05:54.223
A model that wasn't promoted is recorded as not promoted.

47
00:05:54.223 --> 00:06:01.514
And where a document-level overlap turned up in an earlier model line, we removed the affected evaluation rows and said so.

48
00:06:01.514 --> 00:06:10.760
Every figure carries a label: real or constructed material, and which serving stack produced it. A result is only worth what the test could have done to it.

49
00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:23.504
On the real court items, the rule returned a proof on none of two hundred ninety court-rejected items, one-sided ninety-five percent upper bound one point oh three percent, checked after the fact on the mixed stack.

50
00:06:23.504 --> 00:06:30.380
It also proved none of thirty-one real court-established items that reached the judges, and five more were referred.

51
00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:39.152
If the two rows on which the second judge was unavailable are counted as proofs, the count is two of two hundred ninety, two point one five percent.

52
00:06:39.152 --> 00:06:42.175
Head-to-head against a frontier model is not shown.

53
00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:48.125
Built to show its work, and to say so when the facts don't carry it.
